# quests store snapshot (internal/quests)
# Built 2026-09-12 from PRE-migration code, sending what each site sends today:
# rewards playermessage/roommessage and action send_text RAW (no substitution),
# action room_text through textutil.SubstituteTokens with the player as {source}.
# dimensions: quest id x rewards | trigger<i>|action<j>[|sequence|action<k>...] x key

quest|2|rewards|playermessage => The chieftain inclines its heavy head -- the closest thing to a bow its mutated frame allows. You are not welcome here. But you are tolerated. That is more than any surface-dweller has been given in living memory.
quest|2|rewards|roommessage => A low, guttural exchange passes between the shaman and the chieftain. Something has shifted.
quest|4|rewards|playermessage => Tessara examines the letter with a grim expression, then folds it carefully into her report stack. 'This is exactly what I needed. Names, routes, schedules -- the wardens at Watchers Crossing will want to see this.' She reaches into a pouch and produces a clay token, pressing it into your hand. 'You have earned this. Show it at any warden post and you will be treated as a friend.'
quest|4|rewards|roommessage => Tessara nods to herself as she reads through the confiscated documents, already composing her report.
quest|5|rewards|playermessage => Tolva pages through the ledger with practiced speed, her lips pressed thin. 'Just as I thought. No writ of authority, no commission from Thornwall, no charter. He set this toll up himself.' She closes the book firmly. 'I will send this to the magistrate with my next supply order. You have done this settlement a service.' She slides a wooden token across the counter.
quest|5|rewards|roommessage => Tolva examines the ledger with grim satisfaction, already composing a letter in her head.
quest|6|rewards|playermessage => Harn exhales slowly when you confirm the report is on its way. 'That is all I can do. If they see the numbers -- what the stone costs, what the masons charge, what happens if that crack reaches the keystone -- they will understand. The toll is not greed. It is the only thing keeping that bridge standing.' He produces a stamped receipt. 'For your crossing. You have earned it, and then some.'
quest|6|rewards|roommessage => Harn nods with quiet relief, adjusting the ledger on his counter.
quest|7|rewards|playermessage => Dorn examines the pest sample with a grim expression, then sets it down carefully. 'Bigger than they should be. The fallow field is feeding them -- all that untended root growth, it is a paradise for vermin.' He looks toward Thornwall's walls. 'Gareth was a good farmer. The tax took everything. And now the rest of us pay for it in different coin.' He reaches into a pouch. 'This is not much, but it is honest.'
quest|7|rewards|roommessage => Dorn nods slowly, the weight of the situation evident in his weathered face.
quest|8|rewards|playermessage => Velk listens to your report without interruption, his expression unreadable. When you finish, he makes a notation in the duty log. 'The case is closed. I will file it accordingly.' He does not ask for details beyond what you offered, and you get the sense that he has seen enough of these cases to know that the paperwork rarely captures the whole truth.
quest|8|rewards|roommessage => Velk makes a notation in the duty log and files the report.
quest|9|rewards|playermessage => The ledger's contents are damning in both directions. Whoever receives it will use it to their advantage, and the other side will suffer for it. Or perhaps the public airing of both parties' dishonesty will force a more honest arrangement. In Thornwall, optimism is a luxury, but sometimes the truth is enough.
quest|9|rewards|roommessage => The tithe ledger changes hands, and the balance of power shifts.
quest|10|rewards|playermessage => Marek pours you a drink on the house -- the good stuff, from the bottle he keeps behind the keg. 'The guard cannot fix the taxes, but at least I will not have to pay two masters. That is something.' He glances toward the cellar stairs. 'While you were gone, I heard noises down there again. Something in the old drainage tunnels beneath the city. I have been meaning to look, but...' He trails off. 'Another time, perhaps. For now, drink.'
quest|10|rewards|roommessage => Marek pours a drink with visible relief, some of the tension leaving his shoulders.
quest|11|rewards|playermessage => You have chosen your path. The steppe will remember.
quest|12|rewards|playermessage => Sylara's eyes shine with quiet pride. The steppe wind swirls around you like a greeting, and you feel a new connection to the land -- ancient, vast, and watchful. She presses a bundle of spirit fetishes into your hands.
quest|13|rewards|playermessage => Rhett grins broadly, cradling the extracted crystal like a newborn. He disappears into his work for hours, and when he emerges, he holds a shield of pale blue crystal -- the Windstone Aegis, forged in gratitude.
quest|13|rewards|roommessage => Rhett emerges from his camp, grinning, and presents a shield of pale blue crystal to his companion.
quest|14|rewards|playermessage => Guard Captain Velk nods with grim satisfaction. 'This ledger changes everything. Names, dates, amounts -- enough to end careers and start trials. You have done Thornwall a real service.' He hands you a folded parchment bearing the Watch seal. 'A commendation. It may not buy you a drink, but it buys you goodwill with the guard.'
quest|14|rewards|roommessage => Velk hands over a sealed document with a look of grudging respect.
quest|14|trigger0|action0|send_text => The iron grate covers a dark opening that descends into the old drainage tunnels. You have no reason to go down there.
quest|14|trigger1|action1|send_text => A tally stick -- notched marks recording regular shipments. Someone is running goods through these tunnels on a schedule. The chalk arrows on the walls point south, deeper into the system.
quest|14|trigger2|action1|send_text => You step into a well-lit chamber that serves as the nerve center of the smuggling operation. A man at the table looks up sharply -- this must be the one in charge. A heavy iron strongbox sits beneath his table, locked tight. Whatever proof you need is probably in there.
quest|14|trigger3|action3|send_text => You fit the brass key into the padlock and turn. The lock clicks open. Inside the strongbox you find a slim leather journal -- a bribe ledger, filled with names, dates, and amounts paid to corrupt city officials. This is the evidence you need.
quest|14|trigger3|action4|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> unlocks the strongbox and pulls out a leather journal.
quest|14|trigger4|action0|send_text => The strongbox is locked with a heavy brass padlock. You need a key.
quest|14|trigger5|action0|send_text => The strongbox is open and empty. You have already taken the ledger.
quest|15|rewards|playermessage => Malk examines the crate, running his elongated fingers over the forced lock. 'This is the one. Everything accounted for, near enough.' He reaches into the cart and produces a small purse. 'Road rate from me, any time you need supplies. Better than road rate, in fact. I do not forget a good turn.'
quest|15|rewards|roommessage => Malk examines the recovered freight crate with evident relief, already tallying his inventory.
quest|16|rewards|playermessage => Delia nods slowly, the practical assessment of someone who has weighed a solution and found it sound. 'You have a good eye for problems and their fixes. If you want to learn something about the plants out there, I can teach you. Come back any time.'
quest|16|rewards|roommessage => Delia's expression shifts from concern to quiet satisfaction as the herb supply problem is resolved.
quest|18|rewards|playermessage => What happened on the north road stays on the north road.
quest|19|rewards|playermessage => The lake-caves bounty is closed. A purse changes hands.
quest|19|rewards|roommessage => is paid out the lake-caves bounty.
quest|19|trigger0|action1|send_text => The skitter-shrimp swarm has been broken. The lake-folk's nets will hold longer for it.
quest|19|trigger1|action1|send_text => The drowned hunter is dead. The shallows are meaningfully safer.
quest|20|rewards|playermessage => Ulla unfolds the journal page slowly. She reads it twice. The second time she sets it on her knee and puts both hands flat over it for a long moment, as if to keep it from blowing away. When she looks up, her eyes are wet but steady for the first time in years.

'I knew it was something,' she says quietly. 'I knew it was something he could not say.'

She rises with effort, walks to the workshop stair, and brings down a long wrapped bundle that has been sitting up there for years -- the hook-spear Brindle finished and never delivered. She places it in your hands without ceremony.

'It was meant for someone who would understand. He'd want it to be you.'
quest|20|rewards|roommessage => is given a long wrapped bundle by Ulla, who closes her hand over hers for a moment before letting go.
quest|20|trigger0|action1|send_text => A long tight spiral inside an outer ring, scratched into the iron with a patient hand. The cuts are dark with age -- old work, set where it would not be noticed by anyone who did not know to look. Not a Chrysalis sigil and not a Stillwater fish-mark. The temple priest in town keeps records of older lore; she may know what the symbol means.
quest|20|trigger1|action1|send_text => A long tight spiral inside an outer ring, scratched into the iron with a patient hand. The cuts are dark with age -- old work, set where it would not be noticed by anyone who did not know to look. Not a Chrysalis sigil and not a Stillwater fish-mark. The temple priest in town keeps records of older lore; she may know what the symbol means.
quest|20|trigger2|action1|send_text => The four pillars at the corners of the temple carry the same spiral that Elgar scratched into his bench- vise -- worked into the stone with patience and skill, far older than the present temple. The temple garden behind this room may hold more of the same.
quest|20|trigger3|action1|send_text => The four pillars at the corners of the temple carry the same spiral that Elgar scratched into his bench- vise -- worked into the stone with patience and skill, far older than the present temple. The temple garden behind this room may hold more of the same.
quest|20|trigger4|action1|send_text => The buried marker beneath the crab-apple carries the same spiral. Older than the garden it sits in. There are ruins to the west of town -- an old chapel and a standing-stone circle further on -- where the symbol may appear again.
quest|20|trigger5|action1|send_text => The buried marker beneath the crab-apple carries the same spiral. Older than the garden it sits in. There are ruins to the west of town -- an old chapel and a standing-stone circle further on -- where the symbol may appear again.
quest|20|trigger6|action1|send_text => The buried marker beneath the crab-apple carries the same spiral. Older than the garden it sits in. There are ruins to the west of town -- an old chapel and a standing-stone circle further on -- where the symbol may appear again.
quest|20|trigger7|action1|send_text => The chapel altar carries the same spiral -- the third site you have found. The wardstone circle to the east may be the fourth.
quest|20|trigger8|action1|send_text => The chapel altar carries the same spiral -- the third site you have found. The wardstone circle to the east may be the fourth.
quest|20|trigger9|action1|send_text => The chapel altar carries the same spiral -- the third site you have found. The wardstone circle to the east may be the fourth.
quest|20|trigger10|action1|send_text => The wardstone slab carries the spiral worn deep enough to set a finger into. Standing at the circle's centre and looking eastward back along the route you walked, you can sight the alignment plainly: this slab, the chapel ruin you just came from, the temple's pillars in town beyond it. The four sites form a line. The line continues east of town -- out into the lake, and downward, somewhere only the cave system can reach.
quest|20|trigger11|action1|send_text => The wardstone slab carries the spiral worn deep enough to set a finger into. Standing at the circle's centre and looking eastward back along the route you walked, you can sight the alignment plainly: this slab, the chapel ruin you just came from, the temple's pillars in town beyond it. The four sites form a line. The line continues east of town -- out into the lake, and downward, somewhere only the cave system can reach.
quest|20|trigger12|action1|send_text => The wardstone slab carries the spiral worn deep enough to set a finger into. Standing at the circle's centre and looking eastward back along the route you walked, you can sight the alignment plainly: this slab, the chapel ruin you just came from, the temple's pillars in town beyond it. The four sites form a line. The line continues east of town -- out into the lake, and downward, somewhere only the cave system can reach.
quest|20|trigger13|action1|send_text => The oilskin pouch holds a folded note in Elgar's hand, addressed to Brindle the smith. The note explains the spiral -- alignment points connecting the chapel, the temple, the wardstone circle, and the deep sump beneath the lake. Elgar wrote that he was going down alone because he could not wait for Brindle to finish the work he was doing. The note is dated the day he disappeared. Brindle needs to know you found this.
quest|20|trigger14|action2|send_text => Inside the buried wooden box: a single small carved kingfisher in dark wood -- unmistakably Elgar's work -- and a folded note in a different hand reading 'He was your brother. -- V.' The kingfisher is yours now. Bring it to Vella at the healer's cottage.
quest|20|trigger15|action2|send_text => Inside the buried wooden box: a single small carved kingfisher in dark wood -- unmistakably Elgar's work -- and a folded note in a different hand reading 'He was your brother. -- V.' The kingfisher is yours now. Bring it to Vella at the healer's cottage.
quest|20|trigger16|action1|send_text => There was another small carving wrapped beneath the first -- you can take this one to Vella in place of the one you lost.
quest|28|rewards|playermessage => Dewey's voice stays with you a moment after the grey lets go -- steady, warm, already a little proud. You are awake now, and the world is real.
quest|30|rewards|playermessage => Something under your skin settles into place, the way silt settles in still water. You are not quite who you were when you arrived.
quest|31|rewards|playermessage => Toke presses a small purse into your palm with his upper mouth still talking and the lower one already calling the next notice.
quest|31|trigger0|action1|send_text => A tutor glances up from a slate as you enter. "Newly Opened. When the changes start and you want to know what is happening to you, this hall is where you come."
quest|31|trigger1|action1|send_text => Trader Onna nods as the coin changes hands. "A shirt won't turn a blade, but it beats bare skin. Put it on -- gear does nothing folded in your pack."
quest|31|trigger2|action0|send_text => Trader Onna watches you weigh her wares. "The cotton shirt keeps the chill off and the coulee's teeth out. Type buy cotton shirt -- you've coin enough."
quest|31|trigger3|action1|send_text => You settle the shirt across your shoulders. That is the whole of it -- buy the gear, wear the gear, and the coulee bites a little less.
quest|31|trigger4|action1|send_text => The keeper of the Drowned Lantern tips you a nod as you step in. "First one is on the house -- the coulee's welcome. Rest while it is still being gentle with you."
quest|32|rewards|playermessage => Vorn turns your performance over in his head for a long moment, the way a smith eyes a finished blade for flaws. Then he nods -- once, short, like it costs him something. "Passable. You did not freeze, you did not panic, and you remembered to think between swings. That is more than most manage on the first day." He presses a few coins into your hand. "Take the east path into the wash. Find Garve. He will put that edge to use."
quest|32|rewards|roommessage => Vorn nods at you with a gruff, brief approval.
quest|32|trigger0|action1|send_text => The training dummy shudders and goes still. Your arms remember the rhythm of it -- weight, angle, follow through. Now try something more deliberate: kick dummy or trip dummy.
quest|33|rewards|playermessage => Garve presses a purse into your hand and holds your gaze a moment longer than is strictly necessary -- the look of someone revising their estimate upward. The leggings he slides from the pack beside him are well-made, cut for movement, and they fit.
quest|33|trigger0|action1|send_text => The bandit scout goes down. His gear stays on the body until you take it -- type loot corpse (or get all corpse) to strip it, then wear any armor you pull. One threat in the wash is done. Push deeper toward the cistern for the squatter.
quest|33|trigger1|action1|send_text => The bandit squatter drops -- and he was wearing something worth having. Loot the corpse before you go, then walk back and give Garve the word. The wash is clear down to the cistern.
quest|34|rewards|playermessage => The watch room goes quiet. Whatever the captain was holding this tower for, it is your question now, not theirs. The captain's own blade comes away clean in your hand -- well-kept, perfectly balanced, the only thing in this ruin that was ever cared for. It has changed owners by violence before. It does so again.
quest|34|trigger0|action2|send_text => The fight leaves something behind in your arms and back -- a settled, permanent strength, earned the hard way at the top of a tower.
quest|34|trigger1|action2|send_text => The fight leaves something behind in your hands and feet -- a settled, permanent quickness, earned the hard way at the top of a tower.
quest|35|rewards|playermessage => Rusk takes the dagger and turns it slowly, checking the edge with one thumbnail. The grey forearm catches the forge light. After a moment he sets it back on the anvil and looks at you -- not at the blade, at you -- with the measuring expression of a man deciding whether a piece of iron is worth tempering. "Straight seam. Weight is forward where it belongs. You did not rush the quench." He presses coins into your hand with the finality of a door closing. "West path. Talus slope. Find Ovell. He pulled himself out of a cave-in and has been waiting for someone to notice. Do not make him wait longer."
quest|35|rewards|roommessage => Rusk examines the dagger briefly, nods once, and presses a few coins into your hand.
quest|35|trigger0|action1|send_text => You set to work at the forge. Give it a few moments -- the iron has to take the heat and the shape both. When the finished blade is in your hands, bring it back to Rusk.
quest|35|trigger1|action0|send_text => Rusk takes the blade, turns it once, checks the edge with a thumbnail, and presses it straight back into your hand. "Yours. You made it."
quest|36|rewards|playermessage => Ovell digs a worn leather pouch from his coat with the care of someone parting with something that has been useful. The quartz eye tracks you steadily while the other squints in the slope light. "You cleared it. Pulled the ore. I did not know if you would come back." He presses the component pouch into your hands and curls your fingers around the strap. "Materials go in on their own once you carry it. Sort if you have loose ones already. And if you ever want to break a piece of kit back down -- salvage it. The slope gives back what you put into it, mostly."
quest|36|trigger0|action1|send_text => The scree scavenger goes still on the rock. One threat on the slope is done. Push higher for the lurker.
quest|36|trigger1|action1|send_text => The talus lurker drops back into the rock and does not rise. The ore pocket is yours. Now work the vein.
quest|36|trigger2|action1|send_text => You pry a chunk of basalt-iron from the exposed vein. The ore is dense and dark, shot through with a faint rust-red seam. Bring it back to Ovell.
quest|36|trigger3|action1|send_text => You work a chunk of basalt-iron free from the vein where it crosses the ore pocket. The rock resists, then gives. Bring it back to Ovell.
quest|37|rewards|playermessage => The Den goes still. The beast's fused-stone hide cools under your hand, and the deep vein beyond it catches your light -- a seam of basalt-iron running on into rock no one has worked. You feel the labor of the spoke settle into your arms as a lasting strength, and the shape of a harder recipe settles into your head: you know now how to fold this dark ore into an edge of your own. The blade you carry out is the first of them.
quest|37|trigger0|action1|send_text => The stone-blooded beast shudders, its fused hide cracking like cooling slag, and goes down for the last time. The deep mine is quiet.
quest|38|rewards|playermessage => Birna takes the empty vial back and turns it to the light, checking the film the salve left behind. The green-gold veining in her hands catches the same light. "Clean steep. You did not scorch the root -- most do, the first time." She tucks a pair of empty vials into your pack and presses a folded scrap of waxed paper after them. "The antidote formula. You will want it before the marsh teaches you why. Falv keeps a camp south in the reeds -- he is rough, but he knows the water. Set your home here first so you have somewhere to wake."
quest|38|rewards|roommessage => Birna inspects the empty vial, nods, and tucks a few things into the newcomer's pack.
quest|38|trigger0|action1|send_text => The root grinds to paste, the water draws it green, and the salve seals clean in the vial. Now drink it -- type drink healing salve.
quest|38|trigger1|action1|send_text => The root grinds to paste, the water draws it green, and the salve seals clean in the vial. Now drink it -- type drink healing salve.
quest|38|trigger2|action1|send_text => The salve spreads warm through you, a slow steady mending. That is what a brew is for. Tell Birna how it went.
quest|39|rewards|playermessage => Falv works a leather bandolier off a peg by his fire and tosses it to you underhand, the loops already supple from years of use. The shed-proof skin of his hands pales over the knuckles as he grips. "Wear it. Potions you carry drop into it on their own, and when you drink, the oldest one goes first -- so your brews never sit long enough to turn. You threw clean. You did not blow your own hand off. That is more than most." He nods south, where the reeds go grey. "Past the miasma the water is poison and something rules it. The firebomb formula is yours now, and a tougher brew besides. Make your own pots. You will need them down there."
quest|39|trigger0|action1|send_text => The marsh leech goes limp in the water. First blood in the marsh. Now the firebomb -- get into a fight and throw it.
quest|39|trigger1|action1|send_text => The firebomb bursts in a gout of flame right where you aimed it. Now you feel the cost -- no second trick for a few breaths. That cooldown is shared by spells and special moves too.
quest|39|trigger2|action1|send_text => You work cattail and herb free of the marsh. Good brewing stock. Report back to Falv at his camp.
quest|39|trigger3|action1|send_text => The black pool grows the best of it -- willow-bark and mint, thick at the waterline. Report back to Falv at his camp.
quest|40|rewards|playermessage => The spirit comes apart all at once -- not blood, but a slow collapse, mud and black water and rot falling still and ordinary. Behind where it stood, a spring breaks clear through the rock, and the poison drains out of the air. You feel the long labor of the marsh settle into your body as a lasting toughness, and the shape of its best brew settles into your head: willow-bark and mint and root, the mire's own renewal. Falv was right about the firepots -- and right that you would need them. You carry a brewer's stock out with you, and the recipe to make more.
quest|40|trigger0|action1|send_text => The spirit of the swamp shudders, loses its shape, and sinks back into the mire it came from. The water goes still and, for the first time, clean.
quest|41|rewards|playermessage => Tarn watches you come back with the unhurried attention of someone measuring whether you wasted her time. The amber of her eyes catches a hare's bolt half a field off and dismisses it. "You followed the line instead of staring at your feet. Most never learn that." She works a rolled cloak off her pack and tosses it to you. "Steppe wind cuts after dark. South, then -- Delk hunts the fringe, and the fringe bites back. Tell him Tarn says you can read ground."
quest|41|rewards|roommessage => Tarn looks the newcomer over, nods once, and hands across a rolled traveling cloak.
quest|41|trigger0|action1|send_text => That is tracking -- reading what passed and where it went. The skill sharpens every time you use it, so keep at it. Forage the scrub if you have not yet, then see Tarn.
quest|41|trigger1|action1|send_text => You work the scrub and turn up usable plants. Good steppe forage. Track a hare if you have not, then report to Tarn.
quest|41|trigger2|action1|send_text => The sage flat gives up roots and herbs to a patient hand. Track a hare if you have not, then report to Tarn.
quest|42|rewards|playermessage => Delk turns the cooked meat once, judges it, and grunts -- which from him is praise. The shed-proof skin of his hands shows pale as he pulls the strips apart. "Killed clean, rested rough, ate what you took. That is the whole of it." He pushes a pair of worn field boots across the stone and a scrap of waxed paper after them. "Stew recipe -- a hot meal does more than cold meat. Now the bad work. South, the pack. They run that country in numbers and the alpha runs them. Come back about the pack when your nerve is up."
quest|42|rewards|roommessage => Delk tastes the cooked meat, nods, and hands across a pair of field boots.
quest|42|trigger0|action1|send_text => The pronghorn drops. Take its meat from the carcass -- type get meat -- and you have what you need to cook.
quest|42|trigger1|action1|send_text => You bed down rough and the field does its slow work -- rest mends a body faster than walking does. Type stand when you mean to rise.
quest|42|trigger2|action1|send_text => The meat sears over the fire, salt and smoke and fat. A hot meal in the field is its own small victory. Report to Delk.
quest|43|rewards|playermessage => The alpha goes down hard and final, and with it the will that bound the pack -- the hounds that survive scatter and do not re-form. You feel the long work of the steppe settle into you as a hunter's sharpness, eyes and instinct quicker than they were. From the pelts you took, a jerkin; in your pack, the makings to keep yourself in the field; and in your head, the cut of the hide and how to sew more. Delk was right -- the pack had to be broken at its head, and you were the one who did it.
quest|43|trigger0|action1|send_text => The alpha pack-leader shudders and goes down, and the den falls silent. The pack that ran this country is broken.
quest|44|rewards|playermessage => Grieve watches the mote take your spell and reknit, and something in his mismatched eyes settles. "There. You did not reach for it -- you let it go. That is the whole of it." He takes a long staff of pale oak from the wall and sets it in your hands; it is light, and the wood seems to lean toward the work. "A focus. It will not cast for you, but it will not fight you either. Remember the three channels -- the body's force, the mind's will, the heart's conviction. You loosed conviction just now. Willpower is the stat that shapes a spell, and the more of it you carry, the cleaner the fold. North, to the grove. Orrin will teach you to hold a casting while the world tries to knock it loose."
quest|44|rewards|roommessage => Grieve presses a pale oak staff into the newcomer's hands.
quest|44|trigger0|action1|send_text => Your spell leaves you and breaks against the mote in a flare of folded light; the mote draws inward and reknits, unharmed. That is casting -- will shaped to a purpose and let go. Return to Grieve in the hall below and tell him it is done.
quest|45|rewards|playermessage => Orrin's late-arriving outline catches up to him as he goes still. "You were hit, and the fold held anyway. That is concentration -- not the absence of disturbance, but the holding-on through it." He turns a palm up, and a scatter of weightless sparks gathers above it and disperses. "Here is a wider working, then -- sparks. It is the weakest of the area spells, which is to say it is the first one you can trust yourself with: it touches everything in front of you at once. You loosed it from the same conviction you woke with. Go up and east when you are ready. The scabland is where the grove's trouble comes from -- and where it ends."
quest|45|trigger0|action1|send_text => The drift-wisp comes apart into the motes it was made of and does not gather again. Cast in the grove if you have not, then see Orrin.
quest|45|trigger1|action1|send_text => The grove revenant folds down and is still. Cast in the grove if you have not, then see Orrin.
quest|45|trigger2|action1|send_text => You shape the fold with the fight pressing on you, and it holds. That is concentration. Put down a veil-warped creature if you have not, then report to Orrin.
quest|45|trigger3|action1|send_text => You shape the fold with the fight pressing on you, and it holds. That is concentration. Put down a veil-warped creature if you have not, then report to Orrin.
quest|45|trigger4|action1|send_text => You shape the fold with the fight pressing on you, and it holds. That is concentration. Put down a veil-warped creature if you have not, then report to Orrin.
quest|46|rewards|playermessage => The Unfolded comes apart the way it has always been coming apart, except that this time nothing rises to replace what is lost, and the standing fold sighs out of the world. The thinnest place loosens, the fraying slows, and the air settles to ordinary air. You feel the long strain of holding castings together in the open settle into you as a deeper steadiness of will. And in the quiet, the shape of a new working comes clear -- how to fold not harm but mending, and turn it on your own wounds. You leave the scabland knowing how to heal.
quest|46|trigger0|action1|send_text => The Unfolded unravels a final time and does not refold. The thinnest place falls quiet around you.
quest|47|rewards|playermessage => Wenna watches you a moment with those flat gold eyes, then nods as though settling something. "You ask straight and you listen straight. That is most of getting on with people, and all of getting on with us." She knots a small charm of plaited grass and polished bone and presses it into your hand. "Wear it where it shows. The folk will know the sign and treat you the kindlier for it." A little of her unhurried certainty seems to stay with you as she turns back to the yard. "The stones are west and north of here. Hale keeps them. Ask him the old story -- he has been waiting a long time to tell it to someone who will sit still for it."
quest|47|rewards|roommessage => Wenna presses a small plaited charm into the newcomer's hand.
quest|47|trigger0|action1|send_text => That is the in-game help -- your long-term reference, more patient than any person and always to hand. The folk set their lore in stone for the same reason: so it outlasts the remembering. Return to Wenna when you are ready.
quest|48|rewards|playermessage => Hale's doubled voice settles to a single tone as he regards you. "I felt that from here -- the moment his nerve went. That is rhetoric: not the loud word, but the true one, landed where it weighs most. You have the beginnings of it." He lifts a flat strip of carved bone from among the stones and sets it in your hands. "A reckoning -- the folk's whole story in the old script, small enough to carry. Read it when you have the patience. The oldest end is the part none of us can, any longer." He looks north, to where the ground rises past the ring. "There is a thing the bone only points at. Sere keeps it, at the old shrine. Go up and see."
quest|48|trigger0|action1|send_text => Your words find the gap in his bluster and go straight through it. The fight goes out of Garrow all at once -- he throws up his heavy hands, mutters a surrender, and quits the green. Old Ferd lets out a breath he has been holding for days. Return to Hale; it is done.
quest|49|rewards|playermessage => Sere listens to your account with the stillness of someone who has waited a long time to hear it, the pale shape of her skull faintly lit from within. "So it is real," she says at last, very quietly. "I have felt it under the floor for thirty years and never had the hands or the right to lift it. You did." She does not ask you to bring it to her; she seems to understand it is not a thing to be owned. "Leave it where the light can reach it. Some things are meant to be found more than once." Then, with a gravity that settles over you like a mantle: "You came to us a stranger. You leave a friend of the coulee folk -- and the only living soul who has looked on the thing beneath the shrine and carried the wondering back out. Carry it well. One day, somewhere, it may come to mean more than either of us can see from here."
quest|49|trigger0|action1|send_text => Your fingers find the lifted edge of a flagstone, and under it something that is not stone -- cool, smooth, regular. You work it free: a disc of pale grey metal the size of a spread hand, and nothing about it belongs here. It is neither cast nor forged nor any process the folk have a word for, its face made smooth the way still water is smooth. Fine raised lines branch and rejoin across it with a cold regularity, threading inward toward a single mark pressed deep at the center -- a ring with a line struck through it, the very mark the reckoning-bone carries at its oldest, deepest end. It stays cool in your warm hands. You do not understand it, and you are fairly certain no one in the coulee does. It feels less like something the folk made and lost than like something that was already here when they came, waiting under the floor for a hand to lift it. Now yours has. Bring word of it to Sere.
quest|50|rewards|playermessage => Iden watches the stone bury itself in the straw and gives a single nod. "There. A sling readies itself while your eye is still on the mark, so the only thing you have to think about is the mark. And you have the other half already: a shot from cover lands like nothing else, and it works once, because afterward they know where you are. The eye is the thing that matters now. Not the arm -- the eye. Keep the sling and the shot; they are yours." She tips her head north, toward the canyon mouth. "Bryn holds the slot below the raider bluffs. He will show you the next part -- how to put a shot into something a whole room away, before it ever knows you are there."
quest|50|rewards|roommessage => Marksman Iden nods as the newcomer's shot finds the straw.
quest|50|trigger0|action1|send_text => The stone leaves the sling and strikes the straw with a solid thud. Your hand has already found another and settled it in the cradle. Iden calls down the range: "Now do it again, but from where it cannot see you coming. Type sneak first."
quest|50|trigger1|action1|send_text => You settle out of sight behind the butts. The sling is already loaded, because it loaded itself. Fire from here and see what a shot is worth when nothing is expecting it.
quest|51|rewards|playermessage => Bryn's outline doubles for a moment as he turns to you, then settles. "You felt it, then. The shot lands, and the thing it lands on comes straight up the slot at you -- so the cross-room shot is never free. You buy a hit, and you pay for it with a fight. Worth it, when you choose the ground." He works a compact crossbow loose from the rock behind him and hands it over with a case of bolts. "Heavier than a sling, and it hits like it. Above us is the overlook, and on it a bowmaster named Skell, who has made this whole canyon a killing field from up there. When you have the nerve, the chimney at the canyon's head goes up to him."
quest|51|rewards|roommessage => Scout Bryn passes the newcomer a compact crossbow and a case of bolts.
quest|51|trigger0|action1|send_text => Your shot streaks north out of the Narrows and finds the scout on the open floor beyond. It is hit -- and it is coming. Hold the ground and finish it.
quest|51|trigger1|action1|send_text => The canyon scout drops at your feet, run up the slot by your own shot. That is the whole of it -- reach out, then stand your ground. See Bryn in the canyon mouth.
quest|52|rewards|playermessage => Skell gives ground one step too many and your shot takes him at the lip of the overlook; he goes off the high stand without a sound, and the high ground is yours. You feel the long work of the terraces and the canyon settle into you as a keenness of eye -- distances come quicker now, and the right instant to loose is just there when you reach for it. From the raider cache you take a full hunting bow and ammunition enough to keep it fed. The overlook is quiet. Below, the whole coulee lies open, every spoke of it walked and won -- and past the rim, the wider scablands wait for whatever you have become.
quest|52|trigger0|action1|send_text => Bowmaster Skell goes off the overlook and does not rise. The high ground is yours, and the canyon below falls quiet.
quest|53|rewards|playermessage => Garve counts your tally with a grunt and flips you a few coins. "Good. They breed faster than I can swing. Come back when you have the stomach for more."
quest|53|rewards|roommessage => Garve gives you a short, approving nod.
quest|53|trigger0|action1|send_text => One down. One more should satisfy Garve.
quest|53|trigger1|action1|send_text => The second squatter drops. The wash is clear for now. Garve will have more work when you are ready for it.
quest|54|rewards|playermessage => Ovell nods from his slope, that quartz eye catching the forge-light through the distance. "Still sharp. The iron knows a practiced hand. Come back when you want to keep it that way."
quest|54|rewards|roommessage => Ovell raises a hand from the talus -- a slow, approving gesture.
quest|54|trigger0|action1|send_text => The work rings clean off the anvil. That is what Ovell wanted to know -- that the forge is still yours. Report back to him.
quest|55|rewards|playermessage => Falv flicks a look your way and holds out a hand for the empty vessel. "Still brewing. Good. The marsh does not care how long ago you learned -- only whether your salve is fresh. Come back when the stock runs thin."
quest|55|rewards|roommessage => Falv checks the empty vessel and pockets it without comment.
quest|55|trigger0|action1|send_text => The brew takes shape on the bench -- clean steep, right color. Now drink it down.
quest|55|trigger1|action1|send_text => The brew does what it promised. That is what Falv wanted to know. Report back to him.
quest|56|rewards|playermessage => Delk takes a look at what you brought back and gives a short nod. "Good run. Clean kill, eyes open. The fringe teaches you different things each pass -- come back when you are ready to listen again."
quest|56|rewards|roommessage => Delk inspects your haul with a hunter's brief, approving eye.
quest|56|trigger0|action1|send_text => The pronghorn goes down clean. Now forage the camp -- the fringe always gives something to a patient hand.
quest|56|trigger1|action1|send_text => You pull what the fringe was holding -- root, bark, and something the morning left. That is what Delk meant. Head back to him.
quest|57|rewards|playermessage => Orrin's edge arrives a half-step behind him, as always, when he turns to you. "The fold held clean. The grove is quieter when the work is done properly -- come back when it needs doing again. It always needs doing again."
quest|57|rewards|roommessage => Orrin's outline catches up with him slowly, as if the grove approves of the stillness.
quest|57|trigger0|action1|send_text => The drift-wisp unravels and is gone. The grove is a little quieter. Go back and tell Orrin it is done.
quest|58|rewards|playermessage => Sere listens in her unhurried way as you describe what the stones showed you this time. "It changes," she says. "That is the thing about very old places -- they keep speaking, and the words shift with the listener. Come again when you have patience for it."
quest|58|rewards|roommessage => Sere folds her hands and listens with the patience of someone who has been listening a very long time.
quest|58|trigger0|action1|send_text => The stones give up a little more than they did before -- not much, but something. The mark at the center of the disc catches your eye again, unchanged and patient. Bring word of it to Sere.
quest|59|rewards|playermessage => Bryn watches the range go quiet without moving from her position. "Good reach. Stood when it came. Come back when the eye starts to drift -- it always drifts."
quest|59|rewards|roommessage => Bryn's gaze moves back to the bluffs. The compliment was in the coins.
quest|59|trigger0|action1|send_text => The shot crosses the slot and bites. Now it will come at you -- stand your ground and finish it.
quest|59|trigger1|action1|send_text => The target drops. Shot placed, fight finished -- that is the whole drill. Come back and tell Bryn.
quest|60|rewards|playermessage => The guard breaks the seal, scans the packet, and gives a short nod. 'Came the whole road, did it. And you with it.' He presses a stamped brass token into your hand. 'Couriers' token. The post-houses from here to the toll will know it -- a bed and a meal on the road, and a name that travels ahead of you. The capital remembers who carries its word.'
quest|60|rewards|roommessage => The East Gate guard takes the sealed packet, checks its seal against the gate ledger, and waves the courier through.
quest|61|rewards|playermessage => Pemberton listens to the whole of it, then lets out a breath she seems to have been holding for a week. "Both of them. Then I can tell my lodgers the road north is walkable again and not be lying." She counts coin from the strongbox without haggling for once, and presses a salve into your hand on top of it. "For the next set of bruises. You will have them, doing work like this. Bed and board here are yours at a friend's rate from now on."
quest|61|rewards|roommessage => Goodwife Pemberton settles a purse into the traveller's hand with the air of a woman whose trade just started breathing again.
quest|61|trigger0|action1|send_text => The hedgerow bandit folds into the ditch he ambushed from and does not get up. One of the road's two is done. The Reach-Touched still haunts the Empty Reach to the north.
quest|61|trigger1|action1|send_text => The Reach-Touched shudders, goes still, and is just a body on the stones. The road north is clear of both now. Carry the word back to Pemberton at the Lake & Ladle.
quest|62|rewards|playermessage => Tamsin works the pole-callus on her palm with a thumb and looks south toward the reeds, where nothing is moving for once. "That thing put two of my passengers in the mud this season and near cost me a third. It is a kinder crossing without it." She counts out coin and tucks a small stoppered vial in on top. "Antivenom. Keep it -- the reeds breed more than one of those, and the next might find you before you find it."
quest|62|rewards|roommessage => Tamsin Reed presses coin and a small vial into the traveller's hand, glancing south toward the quiet reeds.
quest|62|trigger0|action1|send_text => The marsh adder coils, strikes once at nothing, and goes limp in the reeds. The south approach is quiet. Carry the word back to Tamsin at the ford landing.
quest|63|rewards|playermessage => The matter settles the way the docks settle things -- quietly, and for good. Hewd will answer for the difference between his two columns, and Teo goes back on the board in the morning. The dockfolk will hear who sorted it, and down here that is worth more than coin -- though there is coin in it too.
quest|63|rewards|roommessage => Word moves along the wharves in the way it does, and more than one dockhand gives the traveller a short, respectful nod.
quest|63|trigger0|action2|send_text => The proud board lifts with a little persuasion. In the gap beneath, wrapped in oilcloth, lies a small tally-book -- and a glance inside is enough. Two columns of numbers for every shipment: the manifest count on the left, the real one on the right. Foreman Hewd has been skimming the difference for a long time, and the shortage he reported was Teo's only by his choosing.
quest|63|trigger0|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> works a loose floor-board free and pockets something wrapped in oilcloth.
quest|63|trigger1|action0|send_text => One floor-board sits a little proud of its neighbors. You could work it loose -- but prying up another man's floor without a reason is a fast way to find trouble you came here to avoid.
quest|65|rewards|playermessage => Coll turns the cold gray lump over in his sweeper's hands, and for once he has nothing to say for a long moment. "Older than the city. Under the city. And it washes up in my gutters when the rain's hard enough to loosen it." He pockets it carefully. "The bookseller wasn't wrong to go quiet. Keep this between us -- the sort who'd want to know about a buried city aren't the sort I'd want knowing I found it. But you did right by an old sweeper, and the quarter remembers that."
quest|65|rewards|roommessage => Coll the sweeper closes his hand around a cold gray lump and, for once, says nothing at all.
quest|65|trigger0|action2|send_text => You set a hand to the seam and the cold of it goes straight to the bone -- not the cold of stone but something older and more patient. A lump comes loose in your fingers, fist-sized and far too heavy: the same gray material Coll finds in fragments, here in the mass, seamed through worked stone that was old before New Plymouth's first wall was laid. The rain loosens pieces of it and the gutters carry them up to the streets above. This is the source.
quest|65|trigger0|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> works a heavy gray lump loose from a seam in the old stonework.
quest|65|trigger1|action0|send_text => A seam of cold gray material runs through the old stone -- not quarried rock, something stranger and far older. You could work a piece loose, but without a reason it is just a curiosity in the dark.
quest|66|rewards|playermessage => Teels is stable -- for now. Ysolde was clear -- the purge holds until the craving comes back, and the craving comes back while the source is still running. The wrapper is gone but the smell stays with you. The docks know where the Bloom comes from, or someone on them does.
quest|66|rewards|roommessage => The traveller looks up from a quiet dockside exchange with the look of someone who has just learned something they cannot unlearn.
quest|67|rewards|playermessage => The trade is broken -- for now. The constable took the case-file and her jaw was set when she read it. Deren stays untouched; the bloodline office is a wall you cannot move with evidence. But the supply stops, and that buys Teels a chance. One life you could reach.
quest|67|rewards|roommessage => A wharfside quiet settles over the traveller, the look of someone who has done what could be done.
quest|68|trigger0|action1|send_text => A floorboard by the cot sits proud of its neighbors. Beneath it, wrapped in oilcloth against the damp, is a torn quarter of a survey in an old, exact hand -- Edvar's work, the map Toby said was hidden here. You ease it free and pocket it.
quest|68|trigger0|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> works a loose floorboard free and lifts something wrapped in oilcloth.
quest|68|trigger1|action0|send_text => One floorboard near the cot sits a little proud of the rest. Prying up the floor of an occupied workshop without a reason is the kind of thing that ends a welcome before it starts. You leave it.
quest|68|trigger2|action5|send_text => You re-cut the old guild-mark over the door until it reads as a working shop again, a cooper's mark a clerk cannot easily call derelict -- and you carry Edvar's survey out to where the circle can keep it safe. Orin presses a copy of the map on you before you go. "The original is below," he says, "where the canal meets the old quarter. Someone outside this should know it is real."
quest|68|trigger2|action6|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> re-cuts a faded guild-mark over a shuttered door until it reads as a working shop again.
quest|68|trigger3|action0|send_text => The cooper's guild-mark is set above the door in faded paint -- a stave and hammer. Re-cutting it would be a real act with real consequences. You do not yet have a reason to.
quest|68|trigger4|action5|send_text => The cooperage will be seized within the quarter, and Toby put out of the only place he keeps. You have what the office pays for discretion -- and the weight of having earned it.
quest|69|rewards|playermessage => Dross lays the rubbing and the reading side by side and goes very quiet, which for him is remarkable. "It holds," he says. "Every line of it holds." He has a proof now that no honest examiner could dismiss -- and he is, you both understand, a man no one with power has ever been obliged to honestly examine. Still. It is known now, and you are one of the few who know it.
quest|69|rewards|roommessage => The traveller has the look of someone who has read something that cannot be un-read.
quest|69|trigger0|action2|send_text => You press paper to the processional relief and work charcoal across it. The figures come up under your hand -- and the longer you look, the less devotional they seem. Each hand is positioned too precisely to be art. Read along the row, they are a sequence: a number, set into stone by someone who wanted it kept where it could not be edited out.
quest|69|trigger0|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> takes a careful charcoal rubbing of the old processional reliefs.
quest|69|trigger1|action0|send_text => The processional figures are very old -- older, somehow, than the plaque that names them. Their hands are oddly placed. Without a reason to study them you move on.
quest|69|trigger2|action0|send_text => Dross takes the rubbing and unrolls it across his knee, tracing the figures' hands. "Yes -- yes, this is the sequence." He rolls it carefully and hands it back. "Keep it. You will need it where I am sending you."
quest|70|rewards|playermessage => Orin turns the rubbing toward the lamp and is quiet for a long moment. "So it is really down there," he says. "The painting copied this, and the cipher counts toward it, and now there is a rubbing of the thing itself. Gritta found it, you stood under it, and I will see it reaches the people who can hold it." He files it away with a care that is its own kind of reverence. You are, without anything being said, part of the quiet now.
quest|70|rewards|roommessage => The old bookseller gives the traveller a slow nod, the kind that settles something unspoken between them.
quest|70|trigger0|action2|send_text => You press paper to the underside of the lintel and bring the symbol up in charcoal -- four concentric rings, eight points at equal intervals, cut by someone who drew it before they carved it. The colony arrived here, found this, and wrote itself into the founding record as first. The rubbing in your hands says otherwise, quietly, the way the stone does.
quest|70|trigger0|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> takes a careful rubbing of the symbol carved beneath the buried lintel.
quest|70|trigger1|action0|send_text => The symbol on the lintel's underside is clean and deliberate -- rings and eight points, cut to be read by someone passing beneath. Without a reason to record it, you only look.
quest|70|trigger2|action0|send_text => The seam of gray material runs along the flood line, wider here than in the galleries -- smooth where the black water works it, taking no mark, no wear. It does not belong to any stone the colony has a name for. It was here before the canal, before the quarter, before the founding the city teaches.
quest|71|rewards|playermessage => Ostry reads the page once, folds it, and puts it somewhere you do not see. "I cannot fight them with this," she says, "and I am not fool enough to try. But I can let the right person know I have it, and tomorrow my deposit comes back and my permit clears, and nobody says why. That is how it works down here." She presses a blade into your hands before you can decline it. "You will get more use from it than from my thanks."
quest|71|rewards|roommessage => Dame Ostry clasps the traveller's forearm once, hard and brief, the nearest thing she has to ceremony.
quest|71|trigger0|action2|send_text => The torn scrap on the floor reads "before the permit clears" -- and the page it tore from is still on the table among the cup-rings, slid half under a ledger-board. You take it: a column of quarterly sums against vendor names, every figure routed up to one unnamed account. It does not prove a crime. It proves exactly where the money goes, and who waits on whom.
quest|71|trigger0|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> palms a page from the table among the cup-rings.
quest|71|trigger1|action0|send_text => A torn scrap on the floor carries four words -- "before the permit clears" -- in a careful hand. There is more to it somewhere, but lifting papers from this room uninvited, before you know whose they are, is the kind of mistake the Gilt Threshold is built to punish.
quest|72|rewards|playermessage => Word of the settling runs the valley before you have even left the lane. Whatever was decided, the shouting at the Golden Bough will quiet now, and that alone has earned you a place at its hearth.
quest|72|rewards|roommessage => The traveller has the settled look of someone who has untangled a thing that stayed knotted for a long time.
quest|72|trigger0|action1|send_text => You set your back to the fallen slabs and haul the slide clear, stone by stone, until the old channel runs open again -- and the water finds its lost path and goes downhill to both farms at once. Neither man gets to be right. The valley does.
quest|72|trigger0|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> hauls a slide of fallen stone clear of an old aqueduct until the water runs through it again.
quest|72|trigger1|action1|send_text => The old water-right is here among the ceremony rolls, dated and witnessed in a careful hand: the head-gate share was set to feed both holdings, turn and turn about. By the letter of it, Ayres has taken more than his due for years. It is not a kind answer. It is the true one, and it favours Fenn.
quest|73|rewards|playermessage => "Three records, three hands, sixty years apart, and they agree on the one thing the official account denies. There was a fourth. Someone unmade it -- not the water, the memory of it." Quist folds a clean sheet and presses it into your hands. "A fair copy, with the fourth channel drawn back where it belongs. Take it. And if you mean to follow this further, the answer is not in any archive of ours -- it is under the temple, in the stone the founders built upon and would not explain. Go carefully. Some questions are settled by people who would prefer they stay that way."
quest|73|rewards|roommessage => Quist presses a folded sheet into the visitor's hands, talking low and urgent.
quest|73|trigger0|action1|send_text => You study the damaged chart past its water-stain. At the junction, plain under the ruined ink, a fourth channel is drawn -- narrow, distinct, running off to the southwest, no mere smear of current. One of the three records Quist sent you to find.
quest|73|trigger0|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> studies the bookseller's water-damaged old chart with new attention.
quest|73|trigger1|action0|send_text => A water-damaged old harbor chart on the bookseller's wall, half its detail lost to a stain. Old, and odd at the junction, but you have no particular reason to study it closely.
quest|73|trigger2|action1|send_text => Tallis's oldest charts show the same fourth channel -- struck out in some copies, re-marked in others, across sixty years of hands that could not agree whether it belonged. The disagreement is consistent. Two records now, and they were never copied from each other.
quest|73|trigger2|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> pores over the oldest charts in Tallis's stall.
quest|73|trigger3|action0|send_text => The oldest charts in the stall, a few showing the river-junction with more channels than the current maps carry. A curiosity of the copying trade -- nothing you have a reason to pursue.
quest|73|trigger4|action1|send_text => The hall's own map carries it too -- a fourth channel pencilled into the margin in an older hand, half-struck-through, the note beside it admitting that the surveyors could not agree. The city's own record, hung in public, says the count was once in dispute. Three independent records. Time to report back to Quist.
quest|73|trigger4|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> examines the marginal note on the great hall map closely.
quest|73|trigger5|action0|send_text => An old marginal annotation on the civic map -- a fourth channel, struck through, with a note that the surveyors could not agree. The staff have stopped seeing it.
quest|74|rewards|playermessage => The Undercroft kept its threshold and gave up its secret: there was a fourth, struck from the sky and the count and the record, and the temple was raised on the place that remembered. What unmade it is not here. The rubbing in your memory points away from the Confluence entirely -- north and older, to wherever the sky was changed. That road is for another day.
quest|74|trigger0|action1|send_text => You read the building-ledger past its careful columns. It accounts for every stone of the temple's raising -- except the lowest courses, which it simply assumes, as though the foundation were a thing received rather than built. The first of the three records.
quest|74|trigger0|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> reads the open building-ledger with sudden attention.
quest|74|trigger1|action0|send_text => An old construction ledger open on a reading-stand, meticulous and dry. Without a reason to read it closely, it is only an account book.
quest|74|trigger2|action1|send_text => The consecration-record sets out the Founders' dedication of the temple -- and its own wording betrays it: they consecrated the site, a word that assumes something already stood here to be made holy. The second record agrees with the first.
quest|74|trigger2|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> studies the framed consecration-record closely.
quest|74|trigger3|action0|send_text => The temple's consecration-record, framed in the Chapter House. A formal dedication in old script -- nothing you have reason to parse word by word.
quest|74|trigger4|action1|send_text => The mason's survey is the plainest of the three: the lower courses of this corridor are older work, it notes, cut and laid by hands the temple's own records do not name. Three records, three different rooms, one conclusion. Time to report to Cael.
quest|74|trigger4|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> examines the old mason's survey pinned in the corridor.
quest|74|trigger5|action0|send_text => A mason's survey-note pinned to the corridor wall, marking the stonework's courses. Technical, and old. You have no particular reason to study it.
quest|74|trigger6|action2|send_text => You read the carving the way the mason's survey taught you to read stone. Four rings, cut with an instrument's care, where every wall above keeps to three. The temple did not miscount -- it chose a count, and built three hundred years on the choice, and set its floor over the wall that remembers otherwise. There was a fourth. The sky the Founders found was not the sky we are taught. You take a rubbing of it -- proof in soft black line -- and the dark keeps the rest of its counsel. Whatever unmade the fourth, the stone does not say. That answer is somewhere else.
quest|74|trigger6|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> takes a careful rubbing from the great carving on the chamber wall.
quest|74|trigger7|action0|send_text => A vast carving of the night sky in nested rings, cut into the chamber's far wall by a very old hand. Without a reason to count them closely, it is only a beautiful, weathered thing in the dark.
quest|74|trigger8|action5|send_text => You hand Aldric the rubbing. He looks at it a long moment, then folds it away with the care of a man filing something he has decided not to act on. You have kept the Keepers' confidence. What that costs, and what it is worth, will be yours to weigh.
quest|74|trigger9|action5|send_text => You give the Margin scholar the rubbing. She holds it like something that might break, then like something that cannot. The truth is out of the temple's keeping now, in hands that will not let it go quiet. You have carried it into the light. Where it leads next is another road.
quest|75|rewards|playermessage => You carry a retired surveyor's directions to a place he would not name. The road runs east into the highland country, older and stranger than the university's quiet maps. Whatever Reth saw out there and chose not to write down is still out there -- waiting in the broken hills. That climb is for another day.
quest|75|trigger0|action1|send_text => You read the survey the way someone looking for what is not said would read it. The site is classified as a mineral deposit in three lines. A survey of that site -- elevations, material samples, seasonal access -- would ordinarily run to a page or more. This one does not. The entry is formal and correct and stops exactly where the detail should get louder. Reth saw something out there, and he filed a paper that buries it. Time to take this back to Brennan.
quest|75|trigger0|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> reads the thin filed survey with sudden, careful attention.
quest|75|trigger1|action0|send_text => An old survey filed under the eastern country, dry and technical. Without a reason to read it closely, it is only a brief, dull entry.
quest|75|trigger2|action4|send_text => You take the marked map from the table — Reth's hand, annotated in the close careful script of a man who does not waste ink. A route east into the highland country, a circled waymark, elevation ticks that tell you what kind of ground you are going into. No name for what is at the end of it. Whatever Reth saw out there he would not write down, and he has not written it down now — but the route is exact, and the final mark is deliberate, and it is enough. The thread runs east.
quest|75|trigger2|action5|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> takes the surveyor's marked map from the table.
quest|75|trigger3|action0|send_text => A retired surveyor's old papers spread across the table — terrain charts and route sketches in a close, careful hand. Without his leave they are not yours to take, and they say little to a stranger at the window.
quest|76|rewards|playermessage => The disc is awake in your hand, warm along its lines, and it answers a shape you have not yet reached. A father's work, finished by a stranger, in the cottage he left to chase it. What it opens waits east, in the broken country, behind a door in the buried face.
quest|76|trigger0|action1|send_text => You work your fingers into the crack and lift the disc free of the floor — cool, too smooth, heavier than its size. Fine raised lines thread across its face to a ring struck through with a line. You do not understand it. You are fairly certain no one here does. You put it away.
quest|76|trigger0|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> works something free of the cracked floor and pockets it.
quest|76|trigger1|action0|send_text => The crack in the floor is empty now. You already carry what lay beneath it.
quest|76|trigger2|action1|send_text => You sit at the cold table and read the man's life backward.
Years of it — a hand that starts careful and turns driven, the
same questions put a hundred ways to one buried thing far to
the east. Something made, he is certain, and old past any
reckoning, and wrong in a way he never quite sets down in
words. He calls it only the thing. The ring struck through with
a line is inked in every margin, worried at like a sore tooth.
Then, near the end, the part you came for without knowing it —
a method, worked out slow and sure across many pages, for
waking the grey disc that is its key, and the marked hearth for
the place to do it. The last line is not about the thing at
all. It is a name, set down once and gently, as if to keep one
soft thing safe. Maren. He went east to finish this. He did not
come back to her.

quest|76|trigger2|action2|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> reads a long while at the cottage table.
quest|76|trigger3|action0|send_text => A field journal lies open on the table, its pages dense with a careful obsessive hand and sketches of a mark you have half-seen elsewhere. Without knowing what you are looking for, it is only a lonely man's long notes.
quest|76|trigger4|action4|send_text => You do as the journal says. You set the disc into the cut
center of the hearthstone, where the marks draw inward to a
single point, and you follow his method through — the order of
it, the patience of it, the small exact things a driven man
learned alone and wrote down so a stranger might one day do
this right. For a long moment, nothing. Then the grey face
warms under your fingers — not with your warmth but its own.
The fine raised lines take the thin light from the shutter gap
and keep it, holding a glow a half-breath past when it should
fade, and the ring-struck mark at the center wakes and steadies,
sure of itself now, sure of where it belongs. The disc is a key,
and it knows its door — east, in the broken country, in the
buried face he gave his years to. His work, done at last, by a
stranger's hand in the cottage he left to chase it. And a name
comes to you off the last page, unbidden. Maren. He kept her,
all the way to the end of himself. You hope, wherever she is,
she never learns how far he went.

quest|76|trigger4|action5|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> does something careful at the marked hearth, and the air goes briefly strange.
quest|76|trigger5|action0|send_text => The marks are cut for a thing you do not have in hand. Without the disc, they are only scratches in old stone. The disc lies in the old Reliquary at Pothole Coulee.
quest|76|trigger6|action0|send_text => Marks are cut into the hearthstone in a deliberate pattern — a method for something, though you cannot read it yet.
quest|77|rewards|playermessage => You know now. It sits in you like a swallowed stone. The sky will never look the same, and neither will the blessed, and neither will you. Be careful who you tell.
quest|77|trigger0|action2|send_text => You stand among the stones and let the light find them, and one
by one they wake — a slow inner glow, a drift of moving marks —
and though you cannot read the tongue, the stones do not need you
to. They show. They lay it out plainly, the whole of it, in
light and in shape, and you understand it the way you understand
a face, all at once and without wanting to.

They show a dark with no floor and no walls, and points of cold
fire strewn across it past all counting, and — falling through
that dark on a long spear of fire — this. This place, whole once,
a vessel. Not built by any hand of the world. Built to cross the
impossible black, and full of people. People who chose to come.
Who left somewhere behind and set out across a distance the mind
slides off of, meaning to make a home on the green world below.
Gaius. Here. This ground you were born to. They came to it. On
purpose. From somewhere else.

The stones show the landing, and the sickness after. A fine thing
in the soil and the water and the air of this world, waiting,
native to it — and the strangers had no armour against it. It
took them. It changed them, root and blood, into something the
world would learn to call blessed. But there was nothing chosen
in it, and nothing holy. The Chrysalis was here before them. The
blessing is a catching thing. It is the world's own fever, and
they were only the first to burn with it.

The stones show the few it could not touch. Not favoured. Not
spared by any will above. Only made, by the blind luck of how
they were built inside, so that the fever found no purchase in
them — and their children after, and theirs, carrying that dumb
accident down the long years until it wore the shape of divine
right. The bloodline. Their immunity was never grace. It was the
cruelest kind of chance, and a whole world knelt to it.

And the stones show one thing more, and hold it, so you cannot
look away. The vessel was not alone. It came in company. The
stones show three more, still whole, still waiting, hung in the
high dark above the green world and turning there, patient, for a
very long time. You have looked up at them all your life. Everyone
has. On clear nights you counted them and called them by the old
names and never once knew you were looking at ships. The moons.
The moons are ships. And they are still full.

The light drifts and settles, and the stones go quiet, having
told you everything. You do not feel wiser. You feel like someone
who has been handed a weight and told it is theirs now, forever.

quest|77|trigger0|action3|room_text => <ansi fg="username">Aliceia</ansi> stands motionless before the wall of moving light, and for a long moment does not seem to breathe.
quest|77|trigger1|action0|send_text => They are only stones again now, dark until the light finds them — but you know what they hold, and you know it holds you. The crossing, the fever, the luck, the waiting lights. You have read it. You will always have read it.
quest|1000000|rewards|playermessage => Congratulations, here's some stuff!
quest|1000000|rewards|roommessage => A Generic Quest has been completed!
